__lsan_enable();\n  ...\n```\nSo yes — if the iteration allocates more than frees, the SAME input is executed a SECOND time! Wireshark dissecting a packet allocates lots of memory (proto nodes use a tree pool that IS reset... hmm — but file-scope wmem allocations are never freed → HasMoreMallocsThanFrees TRUE).\n\nSo: **two executions of the same input**: 1st sets &isDMG; 2nd must reach add_tagged_field. They\'re the same bytes though!! So the SAME input must, on the same dissection path, both invoke common AND then add_tagged_field... but they\'d be in the SAME dissection each time — the stale read needs add_tagged_field AFTER common\'s death. If the input triggers common at time T1 (in iteration k) and add_tagged_field at T2 > T1 but within iteration k, no crash. We need add_tagged_field to be reached when common is NOT in the current call stack... \n\n**AH — WAIT!!! HERE\'S THE KEY: dissect_record\'s exception handling!!** Look:\n\n```\nTRY {\n    call_dissector_with_data(frame_handle, ...);   // frame dissector (data)\n}\nCATCH ... ENDTRY;\nfd->flags.visited = 1;\n```\nand inside dissect_frame: \n```\nTRY { encap dissector... } CATCH_BOUNDS... { show_exception } ENDTRY;\n... postdissectors ...\n```\n\nIf the udp postdissector\'s dissection throws an exception MID-WAY (inside ieee80211 common after p_add), the CATCH shows the exception and dissect_frame RETURNS NORMALLY. The stack frames are unwound — common\'s frame DEAD. p_isDMG remains &isDMG (stale, but file scope has it).\n\nNow iteration 2: same input dissected again: udp → capwap... hmm — same path — the exception would happen again at the same place... The input that throws BEFORE reaching add_tagged_field... we need iteration 2 to REACH add_tagged_field via a DIFFERENT path? Same bytes → same path...\n\nHmm, BUT — **pinfo state changes across iterations!** e.g., udp port dissection state, "wmem packet scope" reset... Actually, dissect state should be identical. Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait — BUT what about **ieee80